From 59cf3694636ebd328118107ea018f371d146ec9c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LegendaryFire <tristan.balon@outlook.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 22:32:06 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Run Inkling 2 (GGUF architecture inkling2) on an engine path
 of its own

Unsloth's Inkling-2-Mini GGUFs (llama.cpp PR #32695, the looped
"inkling2" layout: 48 layer visits over 24 layers, core 6-17 three
times) had no engine path. src/inkling2 follows the PR's graph: the
loop's message controllers, block attention residuals, the
relative-position bias with sinks, SWA, the short convolutions, sigmoid
routing and SITU experts (NVFP4 with per-expert scales), the
soft-capped head. strata switches to it from the file's architecture.

The layers split over the visible GPUs when one card cannot hold them.
Decode uses the native MMVQ and grouped expert kernels (which take SITU
now). A prompt chunk runs attention on the tensor cores (two passes, so no
rescaling; the relative bias of a tile as one small product) and, for NVFP4 and
Q8_0 experts, a pipelined tensor-core expert GEMM that copies the GGUF
blocks a step ahead and decodes them in shared memory, SITU in its
epilogue (other expert types stay on the grouped kernels). The chunk is
the largest of 2048 / 1024 / 512 that fits beside the caches. The
full-attention caches are int8 with a scale per head and token (--kv
fp16 keeps FP16): README perplexity 3.47 vs 3.49 in FP16.

With --batch N (the server's "parallel") the engine runs N lanes: each
conversation has its own session, decode executor and thread, and the
lanes share the weights and the prompt executor, so a conversation
alone decodes at full speed and two decode side by side (2x RTX 3060:
44 tok/s alone, 33 + 33 together). A slot takes the lane that holds
its prompt's start, so moving between the solo path and a slot reads
nothing again.

--kv-resident W tiers the full-attention caches: a visit keeps its last
W cells in a VRAM ring and every cell in pinned RAM. A prompt chunk
reads the RAM part in blocks copied into VRAM a step ahead; a decode
splits it between the GPU (the same blocks) and CPU threads, merged
as split-K partials, the split set from both shares' measured times.
The RAM part is beyond the bias range, so the result is the resident
one (outputs within 1e-6). 125K tokens, W 16K: 12.6 tok/s (7.3 GPU
only, 19.6 all resident). Decode attention is one pass (FlashDecoding): a group of lanes reads
a key's K and V rows together, each lane keeping its query dims and a
running softmax, the groups merged per chunk. All resident: 1K 40.6 ->
44.1 tok/s, 32K 32.5 -> 40.8, 128K 20.5 -> 33.2 (a full-attention
visit's read at 128K 4.6 -> 2.4 ms or less, a sliding-window one 120 ->
53 us). --bench-at N times decode at position N without reading a
prompt.

Checked against llama.cpp at the PR head (plus #32657's gate_up
scales) on UD-Q4_K_XL, 2x RTX 3060: README text perplexity 3.49 here,
3.58 there; layer 0 within 0.02% of an FP64 reference. Prompt about
1100 tok/s at 2.8K-6.5K tokens (32K context), decode about 42 tok/s.
